// Generated from docs/USER_GUIDE.md by scripts/build-guide.mjs. Do not edit.
export default "# Tintwork user guide\n\nTintwork is a layered photo editor that runs entirely in your browser. Nothing is uploaded: images, projects and autosaves stay on your device. It works best in Chrome or Edge on Windows with a mouse; other Chromium browsers, Firefox, Safari and touch devices also work.\n\n## Getting started\n\n- **Welcome screen**: open an image, start a blank canvas, pick a size preset (Full HD, square, 4:5, A4, 24 MP) or reopen a recent file. It appears whenever no document is open.\n- **Open** (Ctrl+O): JPEG, PNG (8- and 16-bit), WebP, GIF, BMP, Tintwork projects, layered PSD/PSB files and camera RAW files (DNG, CR2, CR3, NEF, ARW, RAF, ORF, RW2 and more). You can also drag files onto the window or paste an image (Ctrl+V). Opening several files gives each its own tab.\n- **New canvas** (Ctrl+Alt+N; the browser keeps Ctrl+N for a new window): width, height and background (white, background color or transparent).\n- **Recent files** (File ▸ Open recent): Chrome and Edge remember files you opened or saved.\n- **Install**: in Chrome or Edge use the install icon in the address bar to run Tintwork in its own window, offline.\n\n## The workspace\n\n- **Menu bar** at the top, **tool options bar** under it, **tools** on the left, **panels** on the right (Color, Histogram, Layers, History, Looks), **status bar** at the bottom.\n- **Document tabs** sit above the canvas. Ctrl+F6 and Ctrl+Shift+F6 switch (Ctrl+Tab and Ctrl+Shift+Tab belong to the browser's own tabs), Ctrl+Alt+W closes.\n- **Navigate**: mouse wheel zooms at the cursor, hold Space or middle-drag to pan, Ctrl+0 fits, Ctrl+1 shows actual pixels.\n- Press **?** for every keyboard shortcut and **F1** for this guide.\n\n## Tools\n\n| Tool | Key | What it does |\n|---|---|---|\n| Move | V | Drag the layer, selected layers or selected pixels; arrow keys nudge (Shift = 10 px) |\n| Rectangle / Ellipse select | M / O | Drag a selection |\n| Lasso / Polygonal lasso | L / P | Freehand or point-by-point outline |\n| Magic wand | W | Select by color with a tolerance |\n| Object select | W again | Drag a box around the subject; then +Subject / −Background (or Shift / Alt); Enter or Done keeps; **Done + feather** softens the edge |\n| Crop | C | Drag, choose a preset ratio or size, Enter applies |\n| Straighten | | Draw along the horizon |\n| Brush / Eraser | B / E | Size, hardness, opacity; Shift+click draws a straight line |\n| Clone stamp | S | Alt+click sets the source, then paint |\n| Healing / Spot healing | R / J | Remove blemishes and dust |\n| Fill | G | Flood fill with a tolerance |\n| Text | T | Click to type; click a text layer to edit it |\n| Color picker | I | Alt+click with the brush also picks |\n| Hand / Zoom | H / Z | Pan and zoom |\n\n## Selections\n\n- Hold **Shift** to add, **Alt** to subtract, **Shift+Alt** to intersect, or use the buttons in the options bar.\n- Select ▸ All (Ctrl+A), Deselect (Ctrl+D), Invert (Ctrl+Shift+I), Feather (Shift+F6), Color range, Refine edge (Ctrl+Alt+R).\n- A selection limits painting, fills, adjustments and filters. Feathered selections blend softly.\n- **Object select** (press W twice, or Select ▸ Object select): drag a box around the subject. Tintwork learns the colours inside and outside the box and cuts along the strongest edges between them, then smooths the outline (hair and fur edges get partial selection). After the box, the options bar sticks on **+ Subject** so you can paint missed parts with the mouse without holding Shift; choose **− Background** (or hold Alt) for extras it took. Each stroke refines the result in about a second. **Undo stroke** takes the last stroke back, **Enter** / **Done** keeps the selection, **Done + feather** keeps it and feathers by the current Feather radius (one undo step), and **Esc** cancels. It works on colour and edges only (no machine learning), so it does best when the subject differs in colour from its surroundings; a dark suit against a dark background may need a stroke or two.\n\n## Layers\n\n- **New layer** Ctrl+Alt+Shift+N, **duplicate** Ctrl+J, **merge down** Ctrl+E, **reorder** by dragging or Ctrl+[ and Ctrl+].\n- Each layer has visibility, opacity and a blend mode. Double-click a name to rename it.\n- **Masks**: Layer ▸ Add layer mask. Paint black to hide and white to reveal; adding a mask switches the colours to black and white for you (X swaps them). Press `\\` to switch between mask and pixels; Shift+click the mask thumbnail to disable it.\n- **Groups** (Ctrl+G), **clipping masks** (Ctrl+Alt+G) and **layer styles** (drop shadow, inner shadow, outer and inner glow, stroke, color overlay).\n- **Multi-select**: Ctrl+click and Shift+click layers, then move, transform, align, distribute or group them together.\n- **Live layers** (Filter ▸ Convert to live layer) keep filters editable.\n\n## Adjustments and looks\n\n- **Destructive** adjustments are in the Adjust menu: Brightness/contrast (Ctrl+B), Hue/saturation (Ctrl+U), Levels (Ctrl+L), Curves (Ctrl+M), Exposure, White balance and more. They show a live preview; OK applies.\n- **Adjustment layers** (Layer ▸ New adjustment layer) never change pixels and can be edited later by double-clicking them. On big photos the preview updates at reduced resolution while you drag and sharpens a moment after you stop.\n- **Auto enhance** (Ctrl+Shift+L) and **Auto white balance** add editable layers.\n- **Looks panel**: one-click looks made of adjustment layers; Intensity sets the strength. Save your own looks and import 3D LUT (.cube) files.\n\n## Filters and transforms\n\n- Filter menu: Gaussian blur, Sharpen, Add noise, Vignette.\n- **Free transform** (Ctrl+Alt+T; the browser keeps Ctrl+T for a new tab): scale, rotate, skew, perspective and warp grids. Enter applies, Esc cancels.\n- **Guided upright** (Image menu): drag lines along **two to four** edges that should be vertical or horizontal (flatter than 45° counts as horizontal). After two guides you see a live preview; press **Enter**, click **Apply guides**, or draw a fourth guide to apply (Crop / Fill / Keep from the edges option). Guides **snap** to nearby detected edges when you release the mouse. Extra guides help when verticals and horizontals both need correcting. Undo reverts. For free corner tweaks use Edit ▸ Perspective (a light thirds grid appears while you drag corners). The **Guides** button in the Perspective options bar still draws guides during a live perspective edit.\n- **Auto upright** (Image menu, or the **Auto** button in Edit ▸ Perspective): finds long straight edges that should be vertical and **applies** the correction in one click — a small rotation (if they are all parallel but tilted) or a perspective correction (if they converge). If it finds no vertical edges it levels the horizontals instead. Crop / Fill / Keep run from the edges option; Undo reverts. If Auto upright finds nothing it leaves the photo unchanged (no Perspective session left open). After a successful upright, **Crop edges** keeps the largest opaque rectangle — usually at the photo’s aspect ratio, or a free aspect when that keeps much more of the picture without a wild ratio change. If the edges in your photo are not meant to be vertical (a diagonal or artistic shot), use Guided upright instead.\n- **Upright modes**: the Perspective options bar has **Auto · Level · Vertical · Full** (also in the Image menu as *Upright: level*, *Upright: vertical* and *Upright: full*).\n  - **Level** only rotates, so that horizontal edges (a horizon, a shelf, a roofline seen straight on) are level. A single long horizon is enough. It refuses tilts over 15°, which are perspective rather than a tilted camera.\n  - **Vertical** makes vertical edges upright and parallel (the same as Auto when verticals are found).\n  - **Full** corrects verticals and horizontals together, e.g. a facade photographed from below and to one side. It only uses the horizontal edges when at least three agree and they span half the width; otherwise it says so and does exactly what Vertical does. It also **skips** a correction (or just the horizontals) when that would make the horizontals *worse* than leaving the photo alone — for example a nearly level facade, or a wall of near-vertical diagonal fins with no clear horizontals. On many real photos the result can still be off by a degree or so, so check it and drag the corners if needed. A single long leaning object (a ladder, a lamp post) counts no more than a few shorter edges, so it no longer steers the correction on its own; use Guided upright when many slanted edges compete.\n- After any upright correction, the options bar chooses what happens to the empty corners: **Crop edges** (the default) crops to the largest rectangle with no empty area, **Fill edges** fills them from nearby pixels with a mirror + smooth blend (keeps more texture than a pure smear; 8-bit and 16-bit), or **Keep** leaves them transparent. Image ▸ **Crop away empty edges** and **Fill empty edges** do the same at any time, and the Crop tool's **Fit inside image** button picks the largest crop at the chosen ratio with no empty corners. Common ratios sit as chips on the options bar (**1:1**, **4:5**, **16:9**, **8×10**, **Free**).\n- Image menu: rotate, flip, Image size, Canvas size, Resize to preset, Auto straighten, Auto upright, Guided upright.\n- **Typed values**: every slider in a dialog has a number box next to it. Click it (or Tab to it), type a value and press Enter to apply the dialog.\n\n## Saving, exporting and recovery\n\n- **Save** (Ctrl+S) writes a `.tintwork` project that keeps layers, masks, styles, adjustment layers and 16-bit data. **Save as** is Ctrl+Shift+S.\n- **Export** (Ctrl+Shift+E): PNG, JPEG or WebP with a quality setting. **Size** exports at the original size, a long edge, a width or a percentage, without changing the document. **Resolution** (ppi) is written into JPEG and PNG files so print shops and layout apps see the print size; it starts at the document's resolution (crop presets such as 8×10 in at 300 ppi set it). **Output sharpening** (Off, or Screen / Matte paper / Glossy paper at Low, Standard or High) runs after resize and before any watermark — useful for web or print files; it is an approximate unsharp mask, not a printer profile, and is skipped for 16-bit PNG. Built-in Web and Print presets use Screen Standard and Matte Standard. **Export multiple sizes** writes several sizes at once into a folder or a ZIP.\n- **Export presets**: in the Export dialog, choose a preset (Web, Email, Print, Lossless PNG, or your own) under **Preset**; **Delete** removes one of your own; click **Save…** and type a name to keep the current format, quality, size, resolution, output sharpening and watermark choice. File ▸ **Export with last preset** (Ctrl+Alt+Shift+E) skips the dialog and exports with the last preset or settings you used.\n- **Watermark on export**: tick **Add “…”** in the Export, Export multiple sizes or Batch process dialog, and click **Watermark…** (or File ▸ Export watermark…). Under **Type**, choose **Text** (text, size, colour) or **Logo image**; the dialog only shows the settings for the type you chose. For a logo, click **Choose image…** and pick a PNG, JPEG or WebP; transparent areas of a PNG logo stay transparent. **Logo size** is a percentage of: **Width, % of short side** (the default), **Width, % of image width**, or **Fit inside % of width and height** (the logo is made as large as fits inside a box that percentage of the image's width and height, so a tall logo does not grow too tall). Both types have soft shadow, opacity, position (any of nine: top, middle or bottom × left, centre or right) and margin. The watermark is drawn on the exported file only, never on your document, and scales with each output size (at 16-bit too). It is not added to layered PSD files. A saved export preset keeps its own copy of the watermark, including the logo, so changing the watermark later does not change your presets.\n- **Autosave**: every open document is saved in the browser a few seconds after each change. If the browser or the tab closes unexpectedly, the next start offers to **Restore** it.\n- **Actions and batch**: record steps in the Actions menu and replay them on a folder of images with File ▸ Batch process.\n- **Batch with an export preset**: in File ▸ Batch process, choose one of your export presets (or a built-in one) under **Export preset**. The outputs table is filled with the preset's size, format, quality and resolution, and the preset's own watermark (including its logo) is used. The next batch starts with the same preset, so a repeat batch is: choose the folder, OK, choose where to save. Changing an output row by hand switches the choice back to *(set the outputs below)*; clicking **Watermark…** switches to the watermark you edit there.\n- **Batch remembers its folders**: Batch process remembers the source folder and the **Output folder** between sessions. When you open it, it shows how many images are in the remembered folder and where the files will go (*“name” (last used)*); click **Choose folder…**, **Choose files…**, or **Open tabs** (use the images already open) to set the source, or **Forget folders** to clear the remembered folders. In a new browser session the browser asks once more for permission to read and to save there; if you refuse, nothing is written. If the output folder was deleted or renamed, Tintwork says so and asks for a new one. When the output folder is the source folder, the files the last batch wrote are skipped, so they are not processed again. Browsers without folder access (and Firefox) still save a zip.\n- **Batch again** (File ▸ Batch again, `Ctrl+Alt+Shift+B`): repeats the last batch without opening the dialog — same folders (whole folder), or the same **files / Open tabs** subset on the matching open tabs (by name). If no batch has run yet, matching tabs are closed, or its preset/action/look was deleted, it opens Batch process instead and says why.\n- **Drop files on Batch process**: with the dialog open, drop image files or a folder onto the window to make them the source (other files are ignored and nothing opens as a document).\n- **Soft proof** (View ▸ *Proof: glossy / matte / photo rag / newsprint*): previews on screen how a print compresses your image between the paper's white and the darkest black the ink reaches, so you can see shadows that will block up or a white background that will look grey. *Proof: approximate CMY* shows a greyscale of approximate ink load (not a printer CMYK conversion). *Proof: last used* restores the last paper and ink-warning choice. It changes only the view, never your pixels or exports; *Proof: off* returns to normal. These are typical papers, not your printer's profile (no colour profiles are used), so treat it as a guide. **Proof: ink warning on** overlays magenta where the image would crush past that paper's white/black or exceed an approximate ink limit (still no ICC).\n\n## Camera RAW files\n\nOpening a RAW file (File ▸ Open, drag and drop, Open recent, or double-clicking a file once Tintwork is installed) shows the **Develop** dialog with a live preview:\n\n- **Presets**: choose a saved preset, *Last used settings* or *Default settings* under **Apply**. Type a name and click **Save preset** to keep the current settings (tone curve included); **Delete** removes the chosen preset. Presets are stored in this browser. **Export presets…** saves all of them to a `.json` file, and **Import presets…** adds the presets from such a file (a preset with the same name is replaced), so you can keep a copy or move them to another computer.\n- **White balance**: *As shot* uses the camera's setting, *Auto* measures the photo, *Custom* lets you set **Temperature** (kelvin) and **Tint**. Moving either slider switches to Custom.\n- **Tone**: **Exposure** (in stops), **Highlight recovery**, **Shadows** and **Contrast**. Open **Tone curve** to drag points on the RGB curve or on the red, green or blue curve on its own; the label next to it says whether the curve is straight or edited.\n- **Color**: **Vibrance** boosts muted colours more than colours that are already strong, which is gentle on skin tones. **Saturation** changes all colours evenly; −100 gives black and white.\n- **Sharpening**: **Amount** sets the strength (0 turns it off). **Radius** is the size of the edges that are sharpened, in pixels. **Detail** at low values holds back halos on strong edges, and at high values sharpens fine texture fully. **Masking** at higher values sharpens only edges and leaves smooth areas such as skin and sky alone.\n- **Noise reduction**: **Luminance** smooths grain, **Color** removes coloured speckles. Both are a little slower to apply at full size.\n- **Lens**: the lens name and focal length from the file are shown when it records them. When the file carries correction data (most DNG files from phones and many converters), **Corrections** fixes distortion and corner darkening. Untick it to see the uncorrected image. **Distortion** straightens lines by hand: move it right when straight lines bow outward (barrel), left when they bend inward (pincushion). Tick **Grid** (or press `G`) to show a grid over the preview and line up walls, horizons or door frames against it. **Save for this lens** remembers the value for that lens at that focal length; later photos from the same lens open with it (between two saved focal lengths the value is blended), including in batch processing. **Forget** removes the saved values for the lens. Tintwork does not include a lens database, so these values are your own. **Vignette fix** brightens or darkens the corners by hand.\n- **Color fringes**: tick **Remove chromatic aberration** to measure and remove red, cyan, blue or yellow edges toward the corners automatically; the measured amount is shown next to it. **Red / cyan** and **Blue / yellow** fine-tune it by hand, or work on their own when the box is unticked.\n- **Defringe**: removes purple and green fringes along high-contrast edges, such as branches against a bright sky. **Purple amount** and **Green amount** set the strength (0 is off). The **hue from** and **hue to** sliders below each one choose the range of hues treated as fringe (in degrees). Only colours next to an edge change, so purple or green objects keep their colour. Click **Pick fringe colour** (or press `I`) and then click right on a coloured edge in the preview: Tintwork reads the fringe colour there and sets the matching hue range (and an amount of 50 if it was 0). A spot that is too neutral, or a colour that is not purple or green, is refused with a hint. Tick **Show affected areas** to see a mask instead of the photo: white is untouched, darker (tinted) pixels are the ones having their colour removed. The mask is only a view and is never applied.\n- **Local adjustments** change one part of the photo. Up to eight masks can be added, and they are part of the settings, so presets and re-developing keep them.\n  - **Add graduated** adds a graduated filter: full effect on one side of a band, fading to none on the other, like a darkened sky. Drag its two end points or the middle in the preview.\n  - **Add radial** adds an oval with a soft edge (**Feather**). Drag the centre, or the right and bottom handles to change its size.\n  - **Add brush** (or press `B`) lets you paint the mask with the mouse. Drag over the preview to paint; hold `Alt` while dragging (or click **Erase**) to remove paint. The circle under the mouse shows the brush size, and the dashed inner circle where the soft edge starts. **Size** (or the `[` and `]` keys), **Feather** (soft edge), **Flow** (how much each stroke adds; strokes build up where they overlap) and **Smoothing** (steadies the line: the brush trails the mouse a little) apply to the next stroke. Tick **Auto mask** to keep the paint on areas with a colour and brightness like the spot under the brush: it stops at edges, so you can paint a wall without spilling onto the sky next to it. **Clear strokes** removes all the paint from the mask. Press `B` again to stop painting and use the mouse normally.\n  - The **mask list** shows every mask with a summary of what it does. Click a mask to select it (or click its handle in the preview). Double-click it, click **Rename** or press `F2` to give it a name; press `Enter` to keep the name or `Esc` to cancel. **Duplicate** copies the mask with all its settings, **Invert** applies the adjustment everywhere *except* the mask (for example, a darkened background around a subject), and **Delete** (or the Delete key) removes it. `Esc` deselects.\n  - Each mask has **Exposure**, **Contrast**, **Highlights**, **Shadows**, **Whites** (the brightest tones only), **Blacks** (the darkest tones only), **Temperature**, **Tint**, **Dehaze** (removes haze: more contrast and colour in a misty or washed-out area; negative values add haze), **Saturation**, **Hue shift** (turns the colours by up to 180°, for example orange leaves toward yellow, while greys stay grey), **Clarity** (mid-size local contrast that adds punch, or softens with negative values), **Texture** (fine detail such as skin, leaves or fabric), **Sharpness** (or a slight blur when negative) and **Noise reduction**.\n  - **Shapes** combine more than one shape into one mask. Under *Shapes*, click **+ Graduated** or **+ Radial** to add a shape to the selected mask, then choose how it combines: **Add** includes its area, **Subtract** cuts it out (for example a radial brightening a face, minus the eyes), and **Intersect** keeps only the overlap (a radial limited to the lower half of a graduated filter). Tick a shape's **Invert** to use its outside instead. Shapes apply in list order after the mask's own shape and brush strokes, before the mask's own **Invert** and its range masks. Click a shape in the list to select it and drag its handles in the preview; the outlines are green for Add, red for Subtract and yellow for Intersect, and ✕ removes a shape. A mask holds up to eight extra shapes.\n  - **Live preview**: the preview follows slider drags, handle drags and brush strokes while the mouse is still down. When the full preview is slow (a large preview on the CPU), a half-size draft is shown while you drag and the full preview replaces it as soon as you stop; the label under the preview says *draft* or *preview*, the time it took, and the engine that made it.\n  - **Range masks** narrow a mask down. Tick **Luminance range** to limit it to tones from **From** to **To** (0 is black, 100 is white); **Smoothness** softens the limits. Tick **Color range** to limit it to colours near a **Hue**, within **Range** degrees. Click **Pick** next to either one and then click the photo to set the range from that spot. A range works together with the shape: a graduated filter with a blue colour range darkens only the sky, not a building that reaches into it.\n  - Tick **Show mask overlay** (or press `O`) to see the selected mask in red over the photo, with the ranges and auto mask included. It is only a view and is never applied. Press `O` again to hide it.\n- **Before / after** (or the `\\` key) shows the photo with the default settings so you can compare; click it again to return to your edit. When you re-develop a layer, *before* means the settings it was developed with.\n- **Split** (or the `Y` key) cycles *before* on the left / your edit on the right, then *before* on top / after on the bottom, then off. Drag the divider with the mouse (or arrow keys when it is focused) to move it.\n- **Reset** returns to the defaults; **Use camera JPEG** opens the small, already processed JPEG stored inside the file instead.\n- **RAW file** (when opening): *Embed in the project* (the default) stores a copy of the RAW file in the project, so the project works anywhere. *Link to the file on disk* stores only a reference to the file, which keeps projects small; it needs Chrome or Edge and a file opened with File ▸ Open, Open recent or drag and drop. Your choice is remembered for the next RAW file. A linked layer shows a small ring on its RAW badge.\n- **Open** closes the dialog straight away. A quick half-resolution version appears within a moment with the label *Developing full resolution…*, then the full image replaces it as a new 16 bits/channel document. Developing is split across several background threads, so the window stays responsive; a 24 MP file is usually on screen in one to two seconds after Open.\n\nThe settings you open with become the *last used settings*.\n\n**Re-developing later**: the developed layer keeps the RAW file and its settings, and shows a small **RAW** badge in the Layers panel. Double-click its thumbnail, right-click it and choose **Re-develop RAW…**, or use Layer ▸ Re-develop RAW… to open the Develop dialog again with the settings you used. **Apply** replaces the layer's pixels with a fresh develop from the original file, in full 16-bit quality, as one step you can undo. Crops, image resizes, rotations, flips, straightening and free transforms are kept: the fresh develop is fitted into the layer exactly as it is now. If you painted on or filtered the layer after developing, you are asked first, because re-developing replaces those edits; put retouching on a separate layer to keep it. A layer whose selected pixels were transformed cannot be re-developed in place (Tintwork says so). Project files and autosave keep the RAW file (or, for linked layers, only the link), so a saved project can be re-developed after reopening; this makes the project about as much bigger as the RAW file (RAW files over 200 MB are left out of autosave). If Tintwork cannot decode a file's sensor data (an unusual camera or compression), it opens the camera's embedded JPEG instead and says so. That image is 8-bit and the develop settings do not apply.\n\n**Linked RAW files**: re-developing a linked layer reads the file from disk; the browser may ask once for permission to read it. If the file was moved, renamed or deleted, or the project was opened on another computer or in another browser, the **Linked RAW file not found** prompt names the file and its size; click **Locate file…** to choose it again (you are warned if the name or size differs). Cancel leaves the layer as it is. Layer ▸ **Embed linked RAW file** copies the file into the project so it no longer depends on the file on disk.\n\n**Batch processing** (File ▸ Batch process): **RAW files develop with** chooses the last used settings, the defaults or one of your presets for every RAW file in the batch. A batch does not change your last used settings.\n\n**Faster decoding**: when Tintwork is hosted with cross-origin isolation headers (as the included hosting settings do), the RAW decoder uses several threads and decoding takes about half as long. Otherwise it uses one thread; nothing else changes.\n\n**Offline**: the RAW decoder is downloaded and stored when Tintwork is first loaded, so RAW files open without a connection even if you have never opened one before.\n\n## Layered PSD files\n\n- **Open** a `.psd` or `.psb` file to get its layers, names, opacity, visibility, blend modes, groups, clipping and layer masks, and adjustment layers that Tintwork also has (brightness/contrast, levels, curves, exposure, hue/saturation, invert). 16-bit files stay 16-bit.\n- Some parts are converted: text, smart objects and fill or shape layers become pixel layers using the pixels stored in the file, and layer effects are left off. Blend modes Tintwork does not have use the closest match, and adjustment types it does not have are skipped. A notice lists everything that was converted. If the layers cannot be read, the flattened image stored in the file opens instead.\n- **Export** (File ▸ Export layered PSD…, or PSD in the Export dialog) writes layers, groups, masks, clipping, blend modes, opacity, matching adjustment layers and a flattened copy for other apps. Layer styles are baked into their layer's pixels, text and live layers are written as pixels, and 3D LUT and white balance layers are left out (a notice says which). Batch processing can write PSD too.\n\n## 16-bit editing\n\nOpen a 16-bit PNG, a 16-bit PSD or a camera RAW file, or choose Image ▸ Mode: 16 bits/channel. Painting, adjustments, filters, transforms, groups, masks and every layer style keep 16-bit precision. Export to PNG or PSD keeps 16 bits; JPEG and WebP are 8-bit.\n\n## Large images and memory\n\nTintwork estimates how much memory a document needs (width × height × layers). Very large canvases show a warning; ones too big for the browser are refused, and an image that is too large to open is offered at a reduced size. Close unused tabs and flatten finished groups to save memory. Up to 16384 × 16384 pixels is supported.\n\n## Performance and the rendering engine\n\nView ▸ Rendering engine chooses WebGPU, WebGL2 or the CPU fallback. Help ▸ Run performance benchmark measures your hardware and reports the times. If something renders wrongly, switching to the CPU engine is the safest fallback.\n\n**RAW develop engine** (in the same Rendering engine dialog) chooses where camera RAW files are developed:\n- **Automatic** uses the graphics card when there is one, with WebGPU first and then WebGL2, and the CPU otherwise. It never uses a software (emulated) GPU, because the CPU is faster than one.\n- **GPU (WebGPU compute)** develops the preview, full-resolution 16-bit develops and every mask and filter on the graphics card.\n- **GPU (WebGL2) + CPU filters** develops the preview on the graphics card. Noise reduction, sharpening and the other filters run on the CPU, and full-resolution develops use the CPU threads.\n- **CPU (all cores)** uses the background threads. It is the reference that the GPU results are checked against.\n\nThe results are the same on every engine to within one level in 16 bits for the develop itself. With strong noise reduction or sharpening, a few edge pixels can differ by a few levels, which is not visible. The dialog shows which engine is in use and names the graphics card. If the GPU fails (for example after a driver reset), Tintwork switches to the CPU on its own; the Rendering engine dialog then says why.\n\nTo compare the engines on your computer, open Help ▸ Run performance benchmark and keep **include the RAW develop tests** ticked. It times a preview update while dragging a slider, a preview update while painting a brush mask (both with three masks), and a full-resolution 16-bit develop at each size, on the CPU, WebGL2 and WebGPU. A software GPU is marked as such, because its times say nothing about a real graphics card. **Copy results** copies a text report.\n\n## When something goes wrong\n\n- Errors show a short message at the bottom of the window and your work is autosaved immediately. Reload the page and choose **Restore** if anything looks wrong.\n- Files that cannot be read (damaged or unsupported) are reported by name; nothing else is changed.\n- Recent files that were moved or deleted are removed from the list with a notice.\n\n## Accessibility\n\nEvery control can be reached with the keyboard (Tab and Shift+Tab); the focused control has a visible outline. Press **Alt** on its own (or **F10**) to move to the menu bar, then use ←/→ to choose a menu, ↓ or Enter to open it, ↑/↓ to pick a command and Esc to go back. While a dialog is open, Tab stays inside it. Buttons with only an icon have a spoken name. Dialogs open with the first field focused; Enter confirms and Esc cancels.\n\n## Updates\n\nTintwork works offline after the first visit. When a new version is published, a bar at the top says it is ready: save your work and click **Reload** (or **Later** to keep working; the next start uses the new version).\n";
